Chapter: CoapsEndpoint
  cites Net chapter DtlsEndpoint
  cites Encode chapter CoapEndpoint

 coaps:// is CoAP carried as DTLS application data (RFC 7252 section 9.1).
 This chapter is the composition and nothing else: the CoAP interaction
 machine decides what to send and when to retransmit, the DTLS endpoint
 decides how a datagram is protected, and neither had to learn about the
 other.

 ONE COAP MESSAGE PER RECORD. RFC 7252 section 9.1: CoAP over DTLS is
 carried "in the payload of a DTLS record", one message per datagram,
 because CoAP takes its message boundaries from the datagram and has no
 length field of its own. A composition that concatenated two messages into
 one record would hand the parser a buffer whose tail it cannot see.

 A RETRANSMISSION IS A NEW RECORD. The CoAP layer retransmits the SAME
 datagram, deliberately: RFC 7252 section 4.2 identifies a duplicate by
 message id, which is how the server recognises the retry as the same
 request. The DTLS layer must not repeat itself the same way. RFC 9147
 section 4.5.1's anti-replay window drops a record whose sequence number
 has already been seen, so a cached record replayed on the retransmit
 timer is discarded by the peer before CoAP ever sees it -- and the
 symptom is a request that retransmits four times, is never answered, and
 fails, with the peer reporting nothing at all. Every outbound datagram
 is therefore sealed at the moment it is sent, taking a fresh record
 sequence number from the DTLS endpoint, and no sealed record is stored.

 We say:

Section: Types

  CoapsEp = record {
    xe-coap : CoapEp,
    xe-dtls : DtlsEp,
    xe-read : Integer
  }

  CoapsStep = record {
    xs-ep : CoapsEp,
    xs-out : List (List Integer)
  }

Section: Construction

 The DTLS endpoint handed in is expected to have completed its handshake
 and derived its application traffic secrets; this chapter drives no
 handshake, on the same division of labour that keeps the CoAP machine
 free of crypto.

  coaps-ep-new : CoapEp, DtlsEp -> CoapsEp
  coaps-ep-new (c) (d) = CoapsEp { xe-coap = c, xe-dtls = d, xe-read = 0 }

  coaps-ep-idle : CoapsEp -> CoapsStep
  coaps-ep-idle (ep) = CoapsStep { xs-ep = ep, xs-out = [] }

Section: Sending

  coaps-seal-all : CoapsEp, List (List Integer), Integer, List (List Integer) -> CoapsStep
  coaps-seal-all (ep) (dgrams) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length dgrams then CoapsStep { xs-ep = ep, xs-out = acc }
    else let st = dtls-ep-send-app (ep.xe-dtls) (list-at dgrams i)
    in coaps-seal-all (__record-set ep "xe-dtls" (st.es-ep)) dgrams (i + 1) (acc & (st.es-out))

  coaps-ep-get : CoapsEp, Text, Integer -> CoapsStep
  coaps-ep-get (ep) (path) (now) =
    let cs = coap-ep-get (ep.xe-coap) path now
    in coaps-seal-all (__record-set ep "xe-coap" (cs.cs-ep)) (cs.cs-out) 0 []

  coaps-ep-post : CoapsEp, Text, List Integer, Integer -> CoapsStep
  coaps-ep-post (ep) (path) (payload) (now) =
    let cs = coap-ep-post (ep.xe-coap) path payload now
    in coaps-seal-all (__record-set ep "xe-coap" (cs.cs-ep)) (cs.cs-out) 0 []

  coaps-ep-tick : CoapsEp, Integer -> CoapsStep
  coaps-ep-tick (ep) (now) =
    let cs = coap-ep-tick (ep.xe-coap) now
    in coaps-seal-all (__record-set ep "xe-coap" (cs.cs-ep)) (cs.cs-out) 0 []

Section: Receiving

 The DTLS endpoint accumulates opened application data into one buffer
 reserved to capacity, rather than handing back a record at a time, so the
 CoAP message just delivered is the tail of that buffer past what this
 endpoint has already read. A record that fails to open leaves the buffer
 untouched, the tail is empty, and the CoAP machine is never called --
 which is what a tampered or replayed record must do.

  coaps-ep-recv : CoapsEp, List Integer, Integer -> CoapsStep
  coaps-ep-recv (ep) (rec) (now) =
    let st = dtls-ep-recv-app (ep.xe-dtls) rec
    in let buf = dtls-ep-app-in (st.es-ep)
    in let n = list-length buf
    in if n <= ep.xe-read then coaps-ep-idle (__record-set ep "xe-dtls" (st.es-ep))
    else let msg = coaps-slice buf (ep.xe-read) n []
    in let cs = coap-ep-recv (ep.xe-coap) msg now
    in let ep2 = __record-set (__record-set (__record-set ep "xe-dtls" (st.es-ep)) "xe-coap" (cs.cs-ep)) "xe-read" n
    in coaps-seal-all ep2 (cs.cs-out) 0 []

  coaps-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  coaps-slice (bs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else coaps-slice bs (i + 1) n (list-push acc (list-at bs i))

Section: Accessors

  coaps-ep-done : CoapsEp -> Boolean
  coaps-ep-done (ep) = coap-ep-done (ep.xe-coap)

  coaps-ep-failed : CoapsEp -> Boolean
  coaps-ep-failed (ep) = coap-ep-failed (ep.xe-coap)

  coaps-ep-response-code : CoapsEp -> Integer
  coaps-ep-response-code (ep) = coap-ep-response-code (ep.xe-coap)

  coaps-ep-response-payload : CoapsEp -> List Integer
  coaps-ep-response-payload (ep) = coap-ep-response-payload (ep.xe-coap)

  coaps-ep-retries : CoapsEp -> Integer
  coaps-ep-retries (ep) = coap-ep-retries (ep.xe-coap)

 An interaction that completed over an ANONYMOUS handshake completed
 against whoever answered. The peer's identity is the DTLS endpoint's
 verdict and is asked of it directly, rather than inferred from a CoAP
 response arriving.

  coaps-ep-authenticated : CoapsEp -> Boolean
  coaps-ep-authenticated (ep) = dtls-ep-authenticated (ep.xe-dtls)
